   "date": "2019-06-06T11:44:24Z",
   "subject": "Re: Question about HTMT (semTools)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div><div>Hi, Terrence. Thanks for your reply. The full text of the article is available here (open access): <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11747-014-0403-8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11747-014-0403-8&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423568243000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1fY-_RSQWEbAubCeoZUhRn\">https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11747-014-0403-8</a></div><div>The discussion on geometric means is on page 121. Unfortunately, I cannot judge whether it is a strong argument, sorry. But I noticed several times that the correlation (<a href=\"http://cor.lv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://cor.lv&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423568243000&amp;usg=AOvVaw08VJMzJhWixWoqGYdAh_Xi\">cor.lv</a>) is almost always almost identical to the corresponding htmt value according to semTools. Maybe it is due to the arithmetic mean? Best. Ibrahim </div></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 20px 0\"><div style=\"font-family:Roboto,sans-serif;color:#6d00f6\"><div>&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tjorge...@gmail.com</a>&gt;:</div> </div> <div style=\"padding:10px 0 0 20px;margin:10px 0 0 0;border-left:1px solid #6d00f6\"> <div><div></div></div></div></blockquote>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 20px 0\"><div style=\"padding:10px 0 0 20px;margin:10px 0 0 0;border-left:1px solid #6d00f6\"><div><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><p>I think that Henesler et. al. proposed to use the geometric for the latter. Therefore I am not Sure what has been implemented in semTools?</p></blockquote><div><br clear=\"none\"></div><div>I didn&#39;t write this function myself, but it&#39;s not long if you want to check out the source code.</div><div><br clear=\"none\"></div><div><a rel=\"nofollow\" shape=\"rect\" href=\"https://github.com/simsem/semTools/blob/master/semTools/R/htmt.R\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/simsem/semTools/blob/master/semTools/R/htmt.R&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423568243000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3zcCczVWfplvnP1oTwFrKI\">https://github.com/simsem/semTools/blob/master/semTools/R/htmt.R</a><br clear=\"none\"></div><div><br clear=\"none\"></div><div>Looks like the arithmetic mean is provided using the mean() function.  If you can provide the full reference for the article that advocates (and makes a strong case) for the geometric mean instead, I can easily add that as an optional argument.</div><div><br clear=\"none\"></div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792423568243000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1duABwzKxBUR8DindWN62z\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></div></div></div></blockquote>"
