    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><br>\r\n<div><br>\r\n<blockquote type=\"cite\">\r\n<div>On 6 Jun 2019, at 13.59, Terrence Jorgensen &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tjorge...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div>\r\n<br>\r\n<div>\r\n<div> </div>\r\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">\r\nbut the documentation appears incorrect</blockquote>\r\n<div> </div>\r\n<div>What part?  Should I change the description to read &quot;geometric-mean correlation&quot; instead of the vague &quot;average correlation&quot;?</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<br>\r\n</div>\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div>I think that that would be a better way to explain it. The original poster clearly thought that average is the same as arithmetic mean, which indicates that there is a possibility of misunderstanding what the code does. </div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Saying that the documentation is incorrect was perhaps a bit incorrect. </div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Mikko</div>\r\n<br>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
